    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 232000\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n100 PM MST Sun Aug 23 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Isolated to scattered thunderstorms, some strong to severe, are\n  expected this evening across south-central Arizona. \n\n- Increasingly less favorable conditions for thunderstorms will\n  develop starting Monday with virtually no chances for rain from\n  Tuesday through the rest of the week.\n\n- High temperatures will reach or exceed 110 degrees nearly every\n  day this week and will be near daily records, while morning \n  lows remain unusually warm. \n\n- Extreme Heat Warnings are in effect through Saturday with daily\n  Major to Extreme HeatRisk.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nSome upper level jet support and mid-level gravity waves helped\nspark off a line of high-based scattered showers and storms \nacross southwest AZ this morning. This activity has since weakened\nand dissipated. Surface-based moisture has also increased across\nsouthwest AZ and areas south of I-8. Dew point temperatures at\nmidday in Yuma County to western Pima County were up to 65-75 \ndegrees. This moisture return in southern AZ will likely help\nfuel later afternoon and evening thunderstorms. Overall, model\nguidance still suggests this afternoon through this evening \nbecoming a somewhat busy monsoon period. \n\nOutside of southern AZ, where the best moisture return was, \nboundary layer conditions remain similar to the past couple of \ndays with sub-optimal moisture (mixing ratios around 8 g/kg) and \nlimited instability (few hundred J/kg MLCAPE). Initially through \nmuch of, if not all of, this afternoon storms should favor higher \nterrain areas, including portions of La Paz County, and it may \neven look like it will not become active at all. However, hi-res \nguidance continues to show the best time period for activity \nacross south-central AZ coming around and after sunset. Guidance \nshows storms being a bit slower to develop in Pima County, but \nwhen they do they do they will have the best potential to become \nstrong to severe, tapping into the best moisture and instability. \nThe storms to the south this evening will then be capable of \npushing one or multiple strong and dusty outflows northward into \nsouth-central AZ, which may then trigger additional storms. Some\nhi-res model runs also suggest outflow collisions may occur, with\noutflows also from the north, but confidence in outflows coming\ndown from northern AZ is low. Additionally, there are indications\nthe storms to the south may develop into an MCV and track\nnorthward through Phoenix this evening. Strong to locally severe\nwind gusts will be the primary weather hazards, with very high \nDCAPE values up to 1800-2000 J/kg. Localized flash flooding may \nalso become a threat with any slow-moving storms or training of \nstorms, with the best potential southwest of Phoenix where \nmoisture will be highest.\n\nConvective chances for Monday are even more uncertain as guidance\ngenerally shows at most a 10% chance despite moisture and \ninstability being better than today. However, subsidence will be \nmuch stronger on Monday as the subtropical ridge begins to move \nwestward back into our region. We will have to wait until tonight\nto have a better idea on the storm potential for Monday, but it \ndoes seem to be considerably lower than this afternoon/evening.\n\nTemperatures today will again be right near record highs with\nlower desert readings from 110-115 degrees. The increased boundary\nlayer moisture expected for Monday should hold daytime highs down\na bit with NBM forecast highs currently between 106-111 degrees,\nbut heat indices will mostly match what are expected today.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nForecast attention turns away from monsoon storm potential and\nback toward the extreme heat conditions starting Tuesday. The\nsubtropical high center will continue to shift westward into our\nregion into the middle part of the week with the center likely\nsettling somewhere over southern Arizona from Wednesday through at\nleast Friday or Saturday. Forecast H5 heights are also shown to\nreach 594-597dm for the latter half of the week which places it\njust shy of climatological records for late August. NBM\ntemperature guidance remains quite consistent showing highs rising\ninto a 112-116 degree range for the latter half of this week as\nconditions dry out. Daily temperature records in Phoenix will \ncontinue to be in jeopardy through the entire week, but are likely\nto fall short in Yuma and El Centro. Once the high center moves\noverhead by Wednesday, rain chances will essentially go to 5% or\nless. As of now, conditions for any monsoon storm activity do not\nlook good through next weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1800Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\n \nThe potential for multiple outflow boundaries resulting in sudden,\ngusty wind shifts this evening, as well as SHRA/TSRA developing in\nthe Phoenix Airspace presents the greatest weather issue during \nthis TAF period. Winds will favor E/SE late this morning followed \nlikely by a period of VRB winds before shifting out of a \nprevailing westerly direction by 20-22Z this afternoon. TS over \nthe northern and eastern AZ high terrain are likely to send a \nfirst outflow towards the lower elevations late this \nafternoon/early this evening, which would cause a sudden wind \nshift out of a N/NE'rly direction. A cluster of strong storms is \nthen expected to develop over western Pima, southwestern Pinal, \nand/or southern Maricopa Counties this evening, which would \nlikely send a second, stronger outflow across the terminals from \nthe S/SW. This second outflow may cause temporary surface VIS \nrestrictions due to blowing dust and more prolonged slantwise VIS\nissues if rain does not follow. Chances for SHRA/TSRA remain \naround 20-30% across the terminals this evening, and so have \nincluded this in PROB30 groups between 04-07Z. The wind forecast \nis very low confidence this evening into the overnight hours due \nto the possible influence of multiple outflows, and gusty, erratic\nwinds are expected if SHRA/TSRA develop near the terminals. Cloud\nbases will remain mostly above 10 kft AGL through the period.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo major aviation concerns are expected through the next 24 hours.\nWinds at KIPL will favor southeasterly directions. TS activity \nover the Baja spine may send an southwesterly outflow into KIPL by\nthe late afternoon hours, with a less than 20% chance for direct \nTS impacts. KBLH will swing between south to SW'rly winds, with \noccasional gusts to around 20 kts. There is also a low chance\n(less than 10%) for SHRA/TSRA conditions at KBLH, particularly\nduring the overnight period. FEW-SCT mid and high level cloud \ndecks will be common through the period.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nHot temperatures will prevail through the entire week with shower\nthunderstorm chances mainly today and possibly Monday. Strong \ngusty winds will be possible late this afternoon and evening with \nany thunderstorms, while chances for wetting rains will remain \nbelow 20%. Minimum RHs will improve through Monday with readings \nof 15-25% today and 20-30% Monday, before dropping back into the\nteens into the middle part of the week. Temperatures will be \naround 5-10 degrees above normal, with lower desert highs \neclipsing 110 degrees nearly every afternoon. Outside of \nthunderstorm outflow winds, winds will follow the typical diurnal \ntendencies with some modest afternoon upslope gustiness.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...\nRecord highs:\n\nDate       Phoenix         Yuma         El Centro\n----       -------         ----         ---------\nAug 23   114 in 2011    115 in 2011    116 in 1969\nAug 24   115 in 2020    119 in 1985    117 in 1985\nAug 25   113 in 2011    116 in 1962    117 in 1962\nAug 26   117 in 2011    115 in 2020    115 in 1981\nAug 27   114 in 2023    120 in 1981    117 in 1981\nAug 28   117 in 2023    118 in 2023    117 in 2009\nAug 29   116 in 2023    117 in 1954    117 in 2005\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Saturday for AZZ530>555-559-\n     560.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Saturday for CAZ562-565>567-\n     569-570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Benedict/Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman \nAVIATION...Whittock\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\nCLIMATE...18/Kuhlman\n",
